{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792172612,"nonce":"qOJ8kmrdWPBtpm4FD81eTuuZtj8=","title":"A rounded clip cut by another is squeezed into one rounded rect","message":"User-reported in the blinc_ts wiki: round avatars and rounded images partly scrolled under a scroll view's edge lose their shape, a circle drawn as a squat stadium, rounded corners drawn at the cut. clip_data merges every rect clip into one screen clip: the intersection rect, with each corner's radius kept while the intersection still reaches into the source's rounded corner. When the view's edge cuts a circle, the intersection is shorter than the circle but keeps radii as large as before, so its sides flatten; a rounded rect and a straight cut are not one rounded rect.\n\nFix: for a record that can take a local clip (not shadows) and has no transform, the innermost rounded clip, when the clips outside it cut it, becomes the record's local clip (its own rect and radii in the record's space), and the rest stay on screen. Uncut, nothing changes. A blinc_ts pixel test renders a circle and a squircle with and without a scroll view cutting 30 of their 100px: above the cut, 436 pixels at 1x moved by more than 40 levels before, none after.","files":null}]}